  "Name": "Dobrowolski1970-filterD",
  "Source": "J. A. Dobrowolski, \"Optical Interference Filters for the Adjustment of Spectral Response and Spectral Power Distribution,\" Appl. Opt. 9, 1396-1402 (1970). doi:10.1364/AO.9.001396 - Table I (Filter D), p. 1400; Sec. III.D and Fig. 8, p. 1400",
  "Notes": "Colour-temperature conversion filter, tungsten lamp 2854 K -> 3000 K, for a sensitometer (ASA PH2.2-1966), 4 layers on quartz (Sec. III.D)\nSpecification: Relative transmittance proportional to B(lambda, 3000 K)/B(lambda, 2854 K) (Planck), controlled over 0.35-0.70 um; Fig. 8(a) shows it normalised to ~1.0 at 0.35 um, falling to ~0.71 at 0.70 um (with Wien approximation T(lambda) = exp(0.24535/lambda[um] - 0.7010), our reconstruction of the target, not printed).\nReported: Fig. 8(b): calculated (target - normalized transmittance) stays within about -0.012 .. +0.012 over 0.35-0.70 um (plot readings: ~-0.01 near 0.36-0.37 um, ~+0.01 near 0.47-0.50 um, ~0 near 0.70 um). Fig. 8(c): two experimental filters within about +/-0.025. Normalisation constant not stated.\nNotes: Incident medium air (Table I medium 1.000), quartz substrate n = 1.457. Table lists Substrate, layers 1..N, Medium; layer 1 is next to the substrate (the synthesis program grows the design from the bare substrate), so the order is REVERSED here (layer 1 below = last printed layer, next to the medium). Thicknesses are printed as optical thicknesses n*d in micrometres; they are entered as FWOT at reference_nm = 1000 nm, which is the same number (1 FWOT at 1000 nm = 1 um optical thickness), with constant, non-dispersive, lossless indices exactly as printed. Physical thickness d = OT/n. Normal incidence assumed (not stated). 4 layers, as stated in the text; summed optical thickness of the printed layers = 0.7675 um (no total is printed).\nLayers from Table I (Filter D), p. 1400; Sec. III.D and Fig. 8, p. 1400; layer 1 faces the incident medium.\nNot checked: the paper reports this design only as plots.",
